    "result_text": "All tests pass. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## Change: reject owner creation at the daily limit (429)\n\nThe rule follows the existing \"reject\" pattern in the `POST /api/owners` OfficeFloor pipeline (mirroring `RejectFullOwnerCity`), with one function class, one checked exception, and one escalation handler.\n\n**New files:**\n- `function/owner/RejectDailyOwnerLimit.java` \u2014 counts existing owners whose `registrationDate` equals the server's current date and throws `DailyOwnerLimitException` once that reaches `DAILY_LIMIT` (100). It uses `LocalDate.now()`, the same \"today\" definition `DefaultOwnerRegistrationDate` uses to date new owners, keeping the count consistent with how owners get dated.\n- `escalation/DailyOwnerLimitException.java` \u2014 checked exception (`extends Exception`) so it surfaces on the `throws` clause.\n- `escalation/DailyOwnerLimitExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 responds `429 TOO_MANY_REQUESTS` with a `ProblemDetail`, reusing the shared `ProblemDetails.build(...)` helper.\n- `officefloor/escalation/...DailyOwnerLimitException.yml` \u2014 wires the exception to its handler.\n\n**Modified:**\n- `officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted a `rejectDailyLimit` step right after `validate` (so an invalid body is still a 400 first), governed by the same transaction, flowing into the existing `rejectFullCity`.\n\nThe acceptance test `coreRejectsOverDailyLimit` (create 100 owners dated today, expect the 101st \u2192 429) and the full suite are green.",
